hile the code correctly marks this server-only and checks for DATABASE_URL, if someone accidentally sets NEXT_PUBLIC_DATABASE_URL, credentials would leak to the client bundle. No protection against this human error.\",\n      \"category\": \"secrets\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"riskScore\": 32,\n  \"recommendations\": [\n    \"URGENT: Implement CSV formula injection protection in csvCell() functions. Add prefix escaping for values starting with =, +, @, -, or \\\\t by prepending a single quote or using Tab escaping.\",\n    \"Add explicit requireReviewer() authentication checks to all sensitive GET endpoints (especially /api/sources, /api/conflicts, /api/rulesets) rather than relying solely on proxy.ts middleware.\",\n    \"Sanitize or HTML-escape audit detail strings before any HTML rendering. Use a library like DOMPurify or React's built-in escaping for client-side rendering.\",\n    \"Add environment variable validation to prevent NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefixes on sensitive vars. Consider a build-time check or linting rule.\",\n    \"Validate ENGINE_URL endpoints and consider adding certificate pinning or other protections if communicating with external services.\",\n    \"Review and update dependencies regularly. All current versions appear reasonable, but run 'npm audit' as part of CI/CD.\",\n    \"Consider adding Content Security Policy (CSP) headers to prevent XSS and injection attacks across all responses.\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThe codebase has **good foundational security** with:\n- ✅ Proper authentication via Clerk middleware\n- ✅ Input validation via Zod schemas throughout\n- ✅ Server-only modules protecting secrets\n- ✅ Append-only audit logging\n- ✅ Parameterized database queries (Drizzle ORM)\n\n**Main vulnerabilities** are:\n1. **CSV Formula Injection** (Medium) - The most actionable issue. Fix by prefixing dangerous characters with `'` in `csvCell()`\n2. **Potential XSS in audit details** (Low) - If audit data is rendered as HTML without escaping\n3. **Defense-in-depth** (Low) - Read endpoints rely on middleware auth rather than explicit route-level checks\n\nThe risk score (32/100) reflects that while there are issues to fix, the architecture is generally sound with proper layering of defenses.\n",
